gcm/live-draw: guest pixel, guest sample and physical extents, with checked mapping between them (groundwork for an
internal resolution scale; no change in what is drawn).

Purpose (issue #55, step 1 of the internal resolution scale): live-draw holds every render target at its guest sample
size, the guest's pixels times the antialiasing grid (patch 2088), and the same w x h serve as the surface-cache
identity, the size texture aliases expose, the host allocation and the host raster space. Rendering above the native
resolution needs the host's size held apart from the guest's, and every conversion between them in one place, in one
order, with no silent overflow.

Change:
- libs/video/rsx_internal_scale.h (new, pure C99, no platform, graphics API or game code, so it can be shared with
  other ports): the three extents of a target (guest pixel, guest sample, physical), checked multiplication, endpoint
  addition, point, extent and rectangle mapping, exact sample-to-pixel division, and AA x scale composition. The order
  is fixed: pixels -> samples (AA grid) -> physical (internal scale). Scale 1 is an exact identity. Rectangles map by
  their endpoints, so a non-zero origin or a partial rectangle cannot drift. Results fit in int32; every failure
  (factor 0 or above 16, overflow, right < left, an extent that is not whole pixels) returns a status and leaves the
  output untouched.
- rsx_live_draw.c: ld_iscale(), the internal scale, native (1x1) in this patch. Colour surfaces (surface_t), zeta
  surfaces (zdepth_t) and the shared depth record a physical extent beside w x h, equal to it at 1x; the resources
  are still allocated at w x h. The extent is checked before a resource is made (for zeta before an outgrown one is
  retired); a refusal takes the path of a failed create. Nothing reads the physical extent yet: lookup, aliases
  (2087, 2097), feedback copies, the zeta windows and snapshots (2021, 2022, 2063) keep using w x h.
- The draw path's viewport and scissor, and the clear rectangle (2006, 2089), go through the header. Draw: the viewport
  and the surface rect go to physical by ld_iscale(), the guest's scissor by the AA grid composed with it, and they
  are intersected there (scaling by a positive integer keeps the order, so this is the rectangle of intersecting in
  samples and scaling after). Clear: the scissor to samples by the AA grid, intersected with the clip in samples as
  before, then to physical. A refused scissor clips everything (draw) or clears nothing (clear); a refused viewport
  keeps the sample extent. One log line per refusal, 16 in all, under [iscale]. The lines patches 6052, 6064 and 6069
  hook are kept as they were, so the rest of the series applies unchanged.
- WPOS (2088) and DDX/DDY (2096) take their divisor from the AA grid composed with the native scale. The HLSL text is
  the same as before, so shader hashes, pipeline keys (ld_pso_key_tail) and PSO template records (2071, ld_pt_note)
  do not change. Like any change under libs/video, the patch changes the translator id of patch 6047, so the first
  start of a build with it discards the PSO key file and pipeline library once, as after any update. A packed grid
  above 16 per axis now fails the translation instead of being emitted (the RSX's grids are 1 or 2 per axis).
- Present-time scaling (2062, 2095), FSR 1 (6051) and the pre-HUD target (6052) are untouched.

Verification: tools/runtime/test_internal_scale_map.sh. The mapping at scale 1 is checked to give the same endpoints
as the replaced scissor code for every 16-bit origin against a spread of sizes on the 1x1, 2x1 and 2x2 grids, and the
same WPOS text and derivative scale for every packed grid. It also covers non-zero origins, partial clears, alias
subrectangles, zero and largest extents, overflow, invalid factors and malformed rectangles. Wiring checks: physical
extents are only written, and the pipeline key and template record do not see the scale. rsx_live_draw.c compiled
with MinGW-w64 gcc 16.2 at -O2 -Wall -Wextra with the same diagnostics as before the patch. No game run.

Written for this port; not taken from RPCS3 or any other emulator.

diff -ruN a/libs/video/rsx_internal_scale.h b/libs/video/rsx_internal_scale.h
--- a/libs/video/rsx_internal_scale.h	1969-12-31 16:00:00.000000000 -0800
+++ b/libs/video/rsx_internal_scale.h	2026-11-13 13:21:12.236729800 -0800
@@ -0,0 +1,185 @@
+/* Internal resolution scaling: checked mapping between the three coordinate spaces of a render target.
+ *
+ *  1. guest pixel extent    what the guest program addresses: viewport, scissor, WPOS, e.g. 1280x720;
+ *  2. guest sample extent   the target as the guest stores it, pixels times the antialiasing sample grid, e.g.
+ *                           2560x720 for a 2x1 grid. Surface-cache identity, texture aliases and every guest-visible
+ *                           size stay in this space (or in 1.);
+ *  3. physical extent       the size of the host resource: samples times the internal scale, e.g. 5120x1440 at 2x.
+ *                           Used only to allocate host resources and for host raster state (viewport, scissor,
+ *                           clear rectangles).
+ *
+ * The order is fixed: pixel -> sample (times the AA grid) -> physical (times the internal scale). Both steps multiply
+ * by positive integers, so the composed factor is aa * scale per axis, and a conversion back to pixels (a WPOS read, a
+ * derivative) divides by that same product: floor(p / (aa * s)) == floor(floor(p / s) / aa) for p >= 0.
+ *
+ * Scale 1 is an exact identity in every function: the same numbers come back, so a renderer that routes its existing
+ * conversions through this header at scale 1 produces the same commands as before.
+ *
+ * Rectangles are half-open, [left, right) x [top, bottom), and map by their endpoints, so a rectangle with a non-zero
+ * origin cannot drift: map(right) - map(left) == map(right - left) exactly. A zero width or height is a valid, empty
+ * rectangle (it clips everything); right < left or bottom < top is malformed and is refused.
+ *
+ * Every result fits in a signed 32-bit integer (the rectangle offsets of graphics APIs are signed). Every function
+ * returns a status; on failure *out is left untouched, so a caller decides explicitly what an invalid mapping means.
+ *
+ * Pure C99, no platform or graphics headers and no knowledge of any one game: it can be copied between ports as is.
+ * Host test: tools/runtime/test_internal_scale_map.sh. */
+#ifndef RSX_INTERNAL_SCALE_H
+#define RSX_INTERNAL_SCALE_H
+#include <stdint.h>
+
+#define RSX_ISCALE_COORD_MAX  0x7FFFFFFFu   /* largest coordinate or extent a mapping may produce */
+#define RSX_ISCALE_FACTOR_MAX 16u           /* largest factor per axis, also for a composed AA x scale grid */
+
+typedef enum {
+    RSX_ISCALE_OK = 0,
+    RSX_ISCALE_E_FACTOR = 1,     /* a factor is 0 or above RSX_ISCALE_FACTOR_MAX (or a composed grid is) */
+    RSX_ISCALE_E_OVERFLOW = 2,   /* a product or an endpoint would exceed RSX_ISCALE_COORD_MAX */
+    RSX_ISCALE_E_RECT = 3,       /* right < left, bottom < top, or an input above RSX_ISCALE_COORD_MAX */
+    RSX_ISCALE_E_EXTENT = 4      /* an extent is not a whole number of grid cells (sample -> pixel) */
+} rsx_iscale_status;
+
+typedef struct { uint32_t x, y; } rsx_iscale_grid;     /* a factor per axis: AA sample grid or internal scale */
+typedef struct { uint32_t x, y; } rsx_iscale_point;
+typedef struct { uint32_t w, h; } rsx_iscale_extent;
+typedef struct { uint32_t left, top, right, bottom; } rsx_iscale_rect;
+
+/* The three extents of one target, with the grids that relate them. */
+typedef struct {
+    rsx_iscale_extent pixel;      /* 1. guest pixels */
+    rsx_iscale_extent sample;     /* 2. guest samples = pixel * aa */
+    rsx_iscale_extent physical;   /* 3. host resource = sample * scale */
+    rsx_iscale_grid aa, scale;
+} rsx_iscale_target;
+
+static inline rsx_iscale_grid rsx_iscale_grid_make(uint32_t x, uint32_t y)
+{
+    rsx_iscale_grid g = { x, y };
+    return g;
+}
+
+/* The native scale: 1x1. */
+static inline rsx_iscale_grid rsx_iscale_native(void) { return rsx_iscale_grid_make(1u, 1u); }
+
+static inline int rsx_iscale_factor_ok(uint32_t f) { return f >= 1u && f <= RSX_ISCALE_FACTOR_MAX; }
+static inline int rsx_iscale_grid_ok(rsx_iscale_grid g) { return rsx_iscale_factor_ok(g.x) && rsx_iscale_factor_ok(g.y); }
+static inline int rsx_iscale_grid_is_native(rsx_iscale_grid g) { return g.x == 1u && g.y == 1u; }
+
+/* v * f, checked. */
+static inline rsx_iscale_status rsx_iscale_mul(uint32_t v, uint32_t f, uint32_t* out)
+{
+    if (!rsx_iscale_factor_ok(f)) return RSX_ISCALE_E_FACTOR;
+    const uint64_t p = (uint64_t)v * (uint64_t)f;
+    if (p > RSX_ISCALE_COORD_MAX) return RSX_ISCALE_E_OVERFLOW;
+    *out = (uint32_t)p;
+    return RSX_ISCALE_OK;
+}
+
+/* a + b, checked (an endpoint from an origin and a size). */
+static inline rsx_iscale_status rsx_iscale_add(uint32_t a, uint32_t b, uint32_t* out)
+{
+    const uint64_t s = (uint64_t)a + (uint64_t)b;
+    if (s > RSX_ISCALE_COORD_MAX) return RSX_ISCALE_E_OVERFLOW;
+    *out = (uint32_t)s;
+    return RSX_ISCALE_OK;
+}
+
+/* The composed grid inner * outer (AA grid, then internal scale). The product must itself be a valid factor. */
+static inline rsx_iscale_status rsx_iscale_compose(rsx_iscale_grid inner, rsx_iscale_grid outer, rsx_iscale_grid* out)
+{
+    if (!rsx_iscale_grid_ok(inner) || !rsx_iscale_grid_ok(outer)) return RSX_ISCALE_E_FACTOR;
+    const rsx_iscale_grid c = { inner.x * outer.x, inner.y * outer.y };   /* <= 16 * 16: no wrap */
+    if (!rsx_iscale_grid_ok(c)) return RSX_ISCALE_E_FACTOR;
+    *out = c;
+    return RSX_ISCALE_OK;
+}
+
+static inline rsx_iscale_status rsx_iscale_map_point(rsx_iscale_point p, rsx_iscale_grid g, rsx_iscale_point* out)
+{
+    rsx_iscale_point r;
+    rsx_iscale_status st;
+    if ((st = rsx_iscale_mul(p.x, g.x, &r.x)) != RSX_ISCALE_OK) return st;
+    if ((st = rsx_iscale_mul(p.y, g.y, &r.y)) != RSX_ISCALE_OK) return st;
+    *out = r;
+    return RSX_ISCALE_OK;
+}
+
+static inline rsx_iscale_status rsx_iscale_map_extent(rsx_iscale_extent e, rsx_iscale_grid g, rsx_iscale_extent* out)
+{
+    rsx_iscale_extent r;
+    rsx_iscale_status st;
+    if ((st = rsx_iscale_mul(e.w, g.x, &r.w)) != RSX_ISCALE_OK) return st;
+    if ((st = rsx_iscale_mul(e.h, g.y, &r.h)) != RSX_ISCALE_OK) return st;
+    *out = r;
+    return RSX_ISCALE_OK;
+}
+
+/* e / g, exact: an extent that is not a whole number of cells is refused rather than rounded. */
+static inline rsx_iscale_status rsx_iscale_unmap_extent(rsx_iscale_extent e, rsx_iscale_grid g, rsx_iscale_extent* out)
+{
+    if (!rsx_iscale_grid_ok(g)) return RSX_ISCALE_E_FACTOR;
+    if (e.w % g.x || e.h % g.y) return RSX_ISCALE_E_EXTENT;
+    const rsx_iscale_extent r = { e.w / g.x, e.h / g.y };
+    *out = r;
+    return RSX_ISCALE_OK;
+}
+
+static inline int rsx_iscale_rect_ok(rsx_iscale_rect r)
+{
+    return r.left <= r.right && r.top <= r.bottom && r.right <= RSX_ISCALE_COORD_MAX && r.bottom <= RSX_ISCALE_COORD_MAX;
+}
+
+/* [x, x + w) x [y, y + h), with checked endpoints. */
+static inline rsx_iscale_status rsx_iscale_rect_from_origin_size(uint32_t x, uint32_t y, uint32_t w, uint32_t h,
+                                                                rsx_iscale_rect* out)
+{
+    rsx_iscale_rect r;
+    rsx_iscale_status st;
+    r.left = x; r.top = y;
+    if ((st = rsx_iscale_add(x, w, &r.right)) != RSX_ISCALE_OK) return st;
+    if ((st = rsx_iscale_add(y, h, &r.bottom)) != RSX_ISCALE_OK) return st;
+    *out = r;
+    return RSX_ISCALE_OK;
+}
+
+/* Maps both endpoints of each axis. */
+static inline rsx_iscale_status rsx_iscale_map_rect(rsx_iscale_rect r, rsx_iscale_grid g, rsx_iscale_rect* out)
+{
+    if (!rsx_iscale_grid_ok(g)) return RSX_ISCALE_E_FACTOR;
+    if (!rsx_iscale_rect_ok(r)) return RSX_ISCALE_E_RECT;
+    rsx_iscale_rect m;
+    rsx_iscale_status st;
+    if ((st = rsx_iscale_mul(r.left, g.x, &m.left)) != RSX_ISCALE_OK) return st;
+    if ((st = rsx_iscale_mul(r.top, g.y, &m.top)) != RSX_ISCALE_OK) return st;
+    if ((st = rsx_iscale_mul(r.right, g.x, &m.right)) != RSX_ISCALE_OK) return st;
+    if ((st = rsx_iscale_mul(r.bottom, g.y, &m.bottom)) != RSX_ISCALE_OK) return st;
+    *out = m;
+    return RSX_ISCALE_OK;
+}
+
+/* The three extents of a target held at sample size `sample` (a surface clip, a resource size). */
+static inline rsx_iscale_status rsx_iscale_target_from_sample(rsx_iscale_extent sample, rsx_iscale_grid aa,
+                                                              rsx_iscale_grid scale, rsx_iscale_target* out)
+{
+    rsx_iscale_target t;
+    rsx_iscale_status st;
+    if (!rsx_iscale_grid_ok(aa) || !rsx_iscale_grid_ok(scale)) return RSX_ISCALE_E_FACTOR;
+    if ((st = rsx_iscale_unmap_extent(sample, aa, &t.pixel)) != RSX_ISCALE_OK) return st;
+    if ((st = rsx_iscale_map_extent(sample, scale, &t.physical)) != RSX_ISCALE_OK) return st;
+    t.sample = sample; t.aa = aa; t.scale = scale;
+    *out = t;
+    return RSX_ISCALE_OK;
+}
+
+/* The three extents of a target of `pixel` guest pixels. */
+static inline rsx_iscale_status rsx_iscale_target_from_pixel(rsx_iscale_extent pixel, rsx_iscale_grid aa,
+                                                             rsx_iscale_grid scale, rsx_iscale_target* out)
+{
+    rsx_iscale_extent sample;
+    rsx_iscale_status st;
+    if (!rsx_iscale_grid_ok(aa) || !rsx_iscale_grid_ok(scale)) return RSX_ISCALE_E_FACTOR;
+    if ((st = rsx_iscale_map_extent(pixel, aa, &sample)) != RSX_ISCALE_OK) return st;
+    return rsx_iscale_target_from_sample(sample, aa, scale, out);
+}
+
+#endif /* RSX_INTERNAL_SCALE_H */
